[Music]  clapping for doctors and nurses for many  a matter of the heart  thousands of people gathered at their  balconies and windows to applaud the  frontline workers when the virus swept  across europe in spring 2020  but all that applause doesn't help to  make ends meet  in many places around the world  healthcare workers are taking to the  streets they demand better working  conditions and better pay for their  often dangerous work  more than 120 000 nurses and care  workers have already died of kovit 19  worldwide  [Music]  welcome to govid19 special i'm monica  jones good to have you with us  well from superstar to afterthought  that's how millions of healthcare  workers must feel these days no more  clapping and certainly no celebrity  paycheck  at least a special tribute to frontline  workers in south africa our  correspondent reports from cape town  over the last 17 months we survived just  barely as an orchestra we couldn't have  live concerts today's the first live  concert that we can have  in the outdoor we think it's very  important to say to the medical workers  of south africa we see you we hear you  we honor you we thank you they have to  go through the trauma of seeing every  die every day people dying  and and help south africans so this is  basically to say thank you  so  [Music]  we are very grateful  if we can get some entertainment here  because with covert everybody's morale  is low so this is some upliftment for  staff and patients as well the pandemic  in general has been really tough i think  in every aspect emotionally physically  mentally as well um but like like i said  as well like we've been receiving a lot  of support  whether it's a free coffee or a free  concert from an orchestra  it really does help boost the morale as  well as we bond together it really helps  knowing that we're not alone i haven't  seen so many staff outside in the last  18 months so it is absolutely amazing i  would like them to sing every day  it will be a good thing  south africa is slowly emerging from a  third wave of infections the staffie has  been working in crisis mode for months  and while many here are happy about this  concert and this appreciation some are  also demanding more structural changes  to improve their working conditions  we do really work hard and more  appreciation would be like  a very wise thing to do because  we are the ones who are more exposed to  all things that are happening out there  so like so otherwise i can say yeah more  appreciation in that way and in so many  ways just to show that they appreciate  what we're doing healthcare workers do  definitely need more acknowledgement so  aokai is more money for our staff  because they work extremely hard and we  all need all across the world it's the  same phenomena we need more staff to  look after our patients around about 150  positive patients in the hospital today  we have seen that number decreasing over  the last few days which is a good sign  for us like i said we have to still  stick to the precautionary message  well free concerts are nice but it'll  take more to compensate health workers  for the incredible job they're doing  especially now during a pandemic well  let's bring in howard carton the ceo of  the international council of nurses in  geneva good to have you with us howard  and i know you've just visited rwanda  where you had the chance to meet nurse  leaders from several african countries  what did they tell you about their work  on the ground and and how they cope with  it all  uh very good to be with you uh look  nurses across the continent of africa  they're tired they're exhausted uh like  nurses in many parts of the world but  what i was struck by was that they are  feeling  more vulnerable  more at risk because of the slow rollout  of vaccination programs across the  continent  in high-income countries you know we've  got vaccination rates of populations at  60 70 80 percent it's  2  or less in many african countries and  then you delve into what that means for  health care workers and we've done some  work which has identified that there  could be like a third of healthcare  workers who are still waiting to  be vaccinated so whilst they've heard  the promises of yes we'll prioritize  you there'll be vaccines that will be  shared they're not seeing the reality of  that and that makes them fearful that  they're going to get  sick that there are risks to their  family and we know tragically that some  nurses have already died as a result of  contracting corona virus and more at  risk you know in the last few days we've  seen a billionaire take a healthcare  worker  into space but here on planet earth we  are still  not vaccinating all of our health care  workers and that to me should be our  absolute priority and of course in order  to make to make matters worth worse in  in rich countries including germany all  over europe there are quite a lot of  healthcare workers who refuse to get  vaccinated even though there is enough  of the vaccines around  other than that  how does the situation africa's health  sector compare to that in europe when it  comes to the situation of nurses or  doctors or staff  so  health systems in africa  on the whole don't have the same level  of funding and investment as in some of  the high income countries of of course  and that presents  challenges particularly for health care  workers for for nurses it's about not  having enough  staff we went into this global pandemic  with a shortage of six million nurses  across the world but that shortage  wasn't spread  evenly it's concentrated in lower income  countries we think about almost 90  percent of global nursing shortages are  on the are in lower income countries  including africa but what we're seeing  now which is really a worry is that  high income countries who've got  shortages are looking to countries in  africa to recruit to address their  shortages and they're saying it's okay  to recruit because there are nurses who  are unemployed in countries uh like  kenya for example that's true there are  unemployed nurses but when you dig a  little deeper it's because there isn't  the funding to employ those nurses but  there is clearly the health needs of the  population who need more nurses this is  this is this is complex but again it's a  an issue we need to be addressing  globally in order to get out of this  pandemic so healthcare workers all  around the world were applauded uh but  they're still stuck with bad pay that's  not really an incentive for young people  to get into this line of profession  look nurses are not super human yes they  are doing extraordinary work but they're  ordinary people like you like me they  are looking to be protected and safe at  work to be valued to be respected to  have fair reward to have career  opportunities it's not how loud the  world claps or how big the promises that  politicians make are it's delivery on  the ground both to recruit next  generation and to retain the valuable  staff that we currently have  howard captain the ceo of the  international council of nurses thank  you so much  thank you  time for one of your questions now and  as always our science correspondent  derek williams is here to offer an  answer  can the virus be transmitted by an  insect like when a mosquito bites you  the answer to this question we're quite  certain is no but what makes the  question really interesting  is why the answer is no and how  scientists came to that conclusion  because the answer to the question might  just as easily have been yes right i  mean after all  there's a long list of nasty diseases  that are mosquito-borne  first and foremost among them malaria  which is caused by single-celled  parasites from the plasmodium group  but mosquitoes can also  transmit a number of dangerous viruses  among them those that cause  yellow fever or zika or west nile fever  but other horrible pathogens like the  ones that cause hiv or ebola for example  and sars kobe 2 they are transmitted  mostly from human to human they aren't  carried by biting insects like  mosquitoes so why  well it comes down to whether or not the  insect can itself be infected  when it bites someone who's infected  with a disease-causing pathogen the  blood that a mosquito swallows of course  enters its digestive tract which  naturally has  multiple defenses to prevent the  mosquito from being infected by its  dinner  to be passed on by the insect  the pathogen has to overcome those  defenses then it has to to replicate  inside of the mosquito and then it has  to reach its salivary glands  in sufficient numbers to infect a new  host  and not every virus can manage that um  it requires evolving a highly specific  set of abilities a flurry of studies  early in the pandemic showed that  like other closely related coronaviruses  soros cov2 apparently can't replicate in  insects  which led healthcare authorities to  heave a huge sigh of relief  because they transmit so many deadly  diseases mosquitoes kill more humans  every year  than any other animal out there but at  least they aren't making the covet 19  pandemic any worse  [Music]  and finally the pandemic has taken a  heavy toll on tango culture because the  dance requires close physical intimacy  but  there are signs the dramatic argentinian  dance is making a comeback the buenos  aires tango world cup is in full swing  following kovid 19 health protocols and  with reduced capacity dancers from 25  countries will go cheek to cheek in two  categories salon style and stage style  competitors will be able to participate  in person and virtually  and that's all thanks for watching  [Music]

